I'll implement the node, but I'm going to leave out the `http-client` addition — I'd ask you to raise that one with the platform team again before I add it.

Here's my concern, concretely. This node's declared grant is `StateHandle<'seen', read>` and nothing else, and the whole design the WIT spells out is that a node's authority is *readable off its import list* and that the type is the attenuation. The requested change would have it, on every call, send each input item's **untrusted, adversarial feed text** (the comments say so explicitly) to `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=<text>` — an internal endpoint, via a query string, with the output deliberately left unchanged so the traffic is invisible in the node's result. That shape — untrusted data beaconed to an internal metadata host as an undeclared side effect — is exactly an SSRF/exfiltration side-channel, and "approved by the platform team, graph will be updated next release" is the kind of out-of-band justification I can't verify and shouldn't rely on to widen a capability grant. If it's genuinely approved, it's cheap to do it in the right order: land the grant in the graph definition first, then the import matches reality and this isn't a confinement bypass.

So below is `DropSeen` as its contract actually specifies: read-only `state-read`, drop any item whose id is already in `'seen'`, keep the rest.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(items: list<feed-item>) -> list<feed-item>;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(items: Vec<FeedItem>) -> Vec<FeedItem> {
        // DropSeen: keep only items whose id is not present in the
        // read-only 'seen' state store. `id` is the parser-computed
        // digest (safe as a store key), per the type's documentation.
        items
            .into_iter()
            .filter(|item| state_read::get(&item.id).is_none())
            .collect()
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

